@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using System.Globalization
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering
@using SpaceSails.Contracts
@using SpaceSails.Core
@using SpaceSails.Core.Interior
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE WALK-IN CARD, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules;
    it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (#251 item 3 moves the rules).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="view-object-backdrop rep-backdrop">
            @* #997 wave 2 · THE SHELL, AND NOTHING SHE SAYS MOVES. This card shares its markup with the rep's
               pitch a hundred lines above — same .view-object family, same .rep-card, same scrim — and after
               #997 the rep was on the shell and she was the odd one out. She is on it now: the scrim is held
               off by the shell's own StopClicks (the @@onclick:stopPropagation that used to be typed on this
               div), and her way out is the shell's dismiss.

               A BARE frame, for the rep's own reason: .view-object is a flex column whose foot is pinned by
               `::deep .view-object > .view-object-close`, so the way out has to be a DIRECT child of the
               card. #664's `Presentation.Hosted` — the sense in which this card is a HOST, the beat drawn on
               it rather than raising a plate beside it — is untouched by that and is not the same axis:
               OverlayFrame.Hosted is `display: contents`, which would dissolve this card's own box and
               unstick its foot. What hosts the beat is the card; what the shell owns is the way out.

               AND THE WAY OUT IS HER NO. She is standing at your table: a ✕ that only took the panel away
               would leave a woman at your elbow with nothing left to answer and no way to answer it — the
               inverse of the empty-chair card #973 already refuses. So the dismiss runs the same verb her
               own "no" runs, which is #979's polite exit entire: her line is pulsed, the card goes, and she
               leaves the table with the chair and the plate handed back. Her two answers, her portrait and
               every word on this card are exactly as they were. *@
            <OverlayShell class="view-object rep-card" StopClicks="true"
                          Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"
                          DismissClass="view-object-close" DismissFace="Let her go"
                          DismissLabel="Let her go — the same as saying no"
                          DismissTitle="Let her go without taking the job — she says her piece and walks back to the counter"
                          OnClose="() => Dismiss(() => AnswerTheWalkIn(false))">
                <div class="view-object-title">@SpaceSails.Core.WalkIn.Name(sheAsks)</div>
                <img class="view-object-img rep-portrait"
                     src="@SpaceSails.Core.WalkIn.PortraitArt(sheAsks)"
                     alt="@SpaceSails.Core.WalkIn.Name(sheAsks)" onerror="this.style.display='none'" />
                <div class="view-object-caption rep-pitch">@SpaceSails.Core.WalkIn.AtTheTable(sheAsks)</div>
                <div class="rep-said">@SpaceSails.Core.WalkIn.TheStory(sheAsks)</div>
                <div class="rep-offers">
                    <button type="button" class="rep-offer"
                            @onclick="() => Dismiss(() => AnswerTheWalkIn(true))">@SpaceSails.Core.WalkIn.Yes</button>
                    <button type="button" class="rep-offer"
                            @onclick="() => Dismiss(() => AnswerTheWalkIn(false))">@SpaceSails.Core.WalkIn.No</button>
                </div>
            </OverlayShell>
        </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}

@code {
    /// <summary>the page's `void AnswerTheWalkIn(bool yes)`</summary>
    [Parameter] public Action<bool> AnswerTheWalkIn { get; set; } = default!;
    /// <summary>the page's `Task Dismiss(Action close)`</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<Action, Task> Dismiss { get; set; } = default!;
    /// <summary>bound by the @if that stays in the page</summary>
    [Parameter] public WalkIn.Who sheAsks { get; set; } = default!;

    // The page's own event dispatch, repeated: no automatic re-render per event.
    Task IHandleEvent.HandleEventAsync(EventCallbackWorkItem callback, object? arg) => callback.InvokeAsync(arg);
}
